---
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const issueNumber = '009';
const verifiedDateline = 'VERIFIED MAY 2026 · INAUGURAL EDITION';
const updated = '2026-05-15';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="State of Free Tiers 2026"
  description="Year in review of free-tier developer services: most-improved, biggest cuts, dead services, methodology."
  canonical="https://freestack.is-a.dev/state-of-free-tiers/2026"
>
  <article class="annual-report container-narrow">
    {/* Masthead — Issue №009 dateline, mono uppercase, ink-faint. Matches the index hero bar. */}
    <p class="masthead">
      <span class="issue">Issue №{issueNumber}</span>
      <span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
      <span class="verified-line">{verifiedDateline}</span>
      <span class="rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <time class="updated" datetime={updated}>{updated}</time>
    </p>

    <header class="report-head">
      <p class="rom-eyebrow">
        <span class="rom">§</span> ANNUAL REPORT
        <span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
        State of Free Tiers · MMXXVI
      </p>
      <h1>The free-tier list, kept honest.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        This is the inaugural <em>State of Free Tiers</em> — a year-in-review of what the free tier actually
        looks like in 2026 for developers building on someone else's infrastructure. We tracked 300 services
        across 27 categories, re-verified every pricing page by hand, and kept a diary of what got better,
        what got cut, and what quietly shut down. No affiliate links. No paid placement. No tracking.
        Just the year, with the receipts.
      </p>
    </header>

    {/* II — Big-number stat grid. Same Fraunces big-number pattern as the index hero-stats. */}
    <section class="block stats-block">
      <h2><span class="rom">i.</span> The state of the free tier — by the numbers</h2>
      <p>
        A year of verification work, distilled into five numbers. Each one took roughly twelve
        minutes per service per month to keep honest.
      </p>
      <dl class="report-stats" aria-label="2026 state of free tiers — headline numbers">
        <div class="stat">
          <dt>Services tracked</dt>
          <dd>300</dd>
          <p class="stat-note">Across 27 categories.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="stat">
          <dt>Full pricing breakdowns</dt>
          <dd>271</dd>
          <p class="stat-note">
            Free tier <em>and</em> paid-tier highlights, both verified against the live page.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="stat">
          <dt>Bot-blocked pricing pages</dt>
          <dd>29</dd>
          <p class="stat-note">JS-rendered or anti-scrape — queued for the Playwright verifier.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="stat">
          <dt>Placeholder brand colors</dt>
          <dd>100</dd>
          <p class="stat-note">The remaining design-coverage gap. Logo lookup cascade continues.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="stat stat-wide">
          <dt>Median verification cycle</dt>
          <dd>12<span class="unit">min</span></dd>
          <p class="stat-note">Per service, per month. Multiplied by 300 — that's a workweek.</p>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    {/* III — Most-improved free tiers. 3-5 short editorial blurbs. */}
    <section class="block">
      <h2><span class="rom">ii.</span> Most-improved free tiers — 2026 highlights</h2>
      <p class="section-blurb">
        Five places the free tier got <em>materially</em> better this year — either a new permanent free
        tier, a quota increase, or a generation jump in what you can actually do with the allowance.
      </p>

      <article class="entry">
        <h3>Cerebras</h3>
        <p>
          Cerebras opened a permanent free inference tier with <strong
            >one million tokens per day</strong
          >
          and roughly <strong>2,600 tokens per second</strong> on Llama 3.1 — fast enough that the round-trip
          feels local. For prototyping AI features against a real model with real latency, it is the most
          generous launch of the year. The catch is rate limits per minute; the daily allowance is the
          headline.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="entry">
        <h3>Groq</h3>
        <p>
          Groq's free developer tier sits at <strong>14,400 requests per day</strong> — the most generous
          AI-API quota in our entire catalog, by request count. It's not a trial. It's not credit-based.
          It's a permanent free plan with a posted limit, which is exactly what we ask of an entry before
          we'll call it &ldquo;free.&rdquo; Llama and Mixtral models, hardware-accelerated on their LPU
          silicon.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="entry">
        <h3>Cloudflare Workers AI</h3>
        <p>
          Cloudflare's Workers AI free tier landed at <strong>10,000 Neurons per day</strong> with on-device
          models running at the edge. The Neuron is a synthetic unit, not a token, but in practice it
          covers a meaningful amount of inference for hobbyist and prototype work. Bundled into the same
          dashboard as Workers, R2, and D1 — the easiest end-to-end serverless free stack of 2026.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="entry">
        <h3>DeepSeek</h3>
        <p>
          DeepSeek released a free inference API for its open-weight reasoning models, with no
          credit-card requirement at sign-up. The free tier has rate limits rather than a hard
          quota, which is a different shape than the Groq / Cerebras &ldquo;requests per day&rdquo;
          model — friendlier for chat workloads, less predictable for batch jobs.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="entry">
        <h3>xAI Grok</h3>
        <p>
          xAI began handing out <strong>$25/month in API credits</strong> to developers who opt into data
          sharing for model training. It is, strictly speaking, a trial-credit tier rather than an always-free
          tier, but the monthly recurrence is what makes it interesting — it's closer to a stipend than
          a one-shot voucher. Filed under <code>trial-credit</code> in the catalog with the data-sharing
          caveat in <code>notes</code>.
        </p>
      </article>
    </section>

    {/* IV — Biggest cuts of 2026. Real drift findings from v0.5.0. */}
    <section class="block">
      <h2><span class="rom">iii.</span> The biggest cuts of 2026</h2>
      <p class="section-blurb">
        The other side of the ledger. Five quota cuts and pricing corrections we caught during the
        v0.5.0 verification sweep — published with the receipts so the entry diffs are auditable.
      </p>

      <article class="entry">
        <h3>Mixpanel</h3>
        <p>
          Mixpanel's free tier shrank from <strong>20 million events per month</strong> to
          <strong>1 million</strong> — a 95% cut. The free plan still exists, but it is now a fundamentally
          different product: a stub for evaluation rather than a workable hobby analytics tier. We updated
          the YAML, bumped <code>date_verified</code>, and added a
          <code>notes</code> line so contributors don't re-list the old number from a stale blog post.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="entry">
        <h3>Cloudflare Workers AI (pay-as-you-go)</h3>
        <p>
          A pricing correction rather than a cut: our old entry listed Workers AI overage at
          <strong>$0.50 per 1M Neurons</strong>. The live pricing page reads
          <strong>$0.011 per 1,000 Neurons</strong> — a 22&times; difference. We were under-quoting the
          cost by an order of magnitude. The free-tier number didn't change, but the upgrade-path math
          did, which is exactly the kind of drift the monthly sweep is built to catch.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="entry">
        <h3>POEditor</h3>
        <p>
          POEditor's free tier dropped from <strong>30,000 strings</strong> to <strong>1,000</strong
          >
          — a 97% cut. As with Mixpanel, the plan name is unchanged but the plan itself is no longer the
          same product. We left the entry in the catalog rather than retiring it, because 1,000 strings
          is still a real (if narrow) free tier for very small localization jobs. The
          <code>notes</code> block now spells out the history.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="entry">
        <h3>Argos CI</h3>
        <p>
          Argos renamed its free plan from <strong>&ldquo;Free&rdquo;</strong> to
          <strong>&ldquo;Hobby.&rdquo;</strong> The quota is unchanged. Cosmetically minor, but the rename
          signals direction: &ldquo;Free&rdquo; reads as a permanent floor, &ldquo;Hobby&rdquo; reads
          as a courtesy that could be revisited. Worth flagging now so we're not surprised when the actual
          cut lands.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="entry">
        <h3>Koyeb</h3>
        <p>
          Koyeb moved from an <strong>always-free</strong> small instance to a
          <strong>credit-based</strong> trial model. The new shape gives more headroom up front and less
          long-tail value — the opposite of what we want listed under
          <code>tier_type: always-free</code>. The entry shifted to
          <code>tier_type: trial-credit</code> and the <code>facets.credit_usd</code> field now carries
          the real number.
        </p>
      </article>
    </section>

    {/* V — Dead services, moved to "Services we don't include". */}
    <section class="block">
      <h2>
        <span class="rom">iv.</span> Dead services — moved to &ldquo;Services we don't include&rdquo;
      </h2>
      <p>
        When a free tier disappears entirely — or the service shuts down — we don't delete the
        entry. We move it to a historical list in <code>README.md</code> with a one-line reason. The history
        matters: it keeps future contributors from re-listing a dead service from a stale blog post or
        an old &ldquo;best free X&rdquo; roundup, and it gives readers a sense of which categories churn
        the hardest.
      </p>

      <article class="entry">
        <h3>Tebi</h3>
        <p>
          Tebi was, briefly, a 25&nbsp;GB S3-compatible object storage free tier — and one of the
          better deals in the storage category. In 2026 they pivoted to <strong
            >hospitality point-of-sale software</strong
          > under the same brand, and the developer storage product went away entirely. Moved to &ldquo;Services
          we don't include&rdquo; with the pivot note.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="entry">
        <h3>Adaptable.io</h3>
        <p>
          Adaptable.io shut down in <strong>February 2025</strong>. The platform-as-a-service
          (Heroku-shaped, with a generous free tier) is no longer reachable. The entry stays in the
          historical list as a reference, both for posterity and to head off the inevitable
          &ldquo;why isn't Adaptable on here?&rdquo; issue.
        </p>
      </article>
    </section>

    {/* VI — Methodology. Short. Defers to the methodology page. */}
    <section class="block">
      <h2><span class="rom">v.</span> Methodology</h2>
      <p>
        Every entry has a <code>date_verified</code> field showing the last time we opened the service's
        pricing page and compared it, line by line, to what the YAML claims. The cadence is monthly, scheduled,
        and re-runs end-to-end against the entire catalog. Drift cohorts get tracked in the sprint logs;
        corrections ship as small content PRs against
        <code>src/content/services/&lt;slug&gt;.yml</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Contributors submit changes through three GitHub issue templates: <em>new service</em>,
        <em>pricing change</em>, and <em>takedown / correction</em>. The takedown SLA is
        <strong>48 hours</strong> on business days for the maintainer to respond, and most corrections
        close within a week. The full process — verification cadence, contributor templates, conflict-of-interest
        policy — lives at <a href="/methodology">/methodology</a>, and the trademark / takedown /
        licensing rules live at <a href="/legal">/legal</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* VII — Forward look. */}
    <section class="block">
      <h2><span class="rom">vi.</span> What's coming in 2027</h2>
      <p>
        The biggest piece of work in flight is the <strong>compare-page facets backfill</strong>:
        every service gets typed quota fields (<code>storage_gb</code>,
        <code>requests_per_day</code>, <code>cc_required</code>, <code>trial_days</code>,
        <code>credit_usd</code>, and a dozen more) so the category-compare surfaces can render
        side-by-side numeric comparisons instead of paragraph blurbs. Roughly a 10&times; jump in
        what the compare page can do, once the backfill lands across all 300 entries.
      </p>
      <p>
        Behind that: a <strong>Playwright-based drift verifier</strong> to crack the 29 JS-rendered pricing
        pages the current Lychee-and-curl pipeline can't read; a likely migration from
        <strong>GitHub Pages</strong> to <strong>Cloudflare Pages</strong> (decision pending — the Pages
        build queue has been flaky during release windows); a second maintainer onboarding so the project
        doesn't have a single point of failure; and an
        <strong>issue-form-to-PR automation</strong> so straightforward pricing-change reports turn into
        auto-drafted PRs the maintainer can rubber-stamp instead of re-typing.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* VIII — Colophon. */}
    <section class="block colophon">
      <h2><span class="rom">vii.</span> Colophon</h2>
      <ul class="colophon-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Built with</strong> Astro 6, content collections, Pagefind for search, and native View
          Transitions.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Editorial typography:</strong>
          <em>Fraunces</em> for display and italics,
          <em>Inter Tight</em> for body, <em>JetBrains Mono</em> for the dateline and code.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Licensed:</strong> source code under <a
            href="https://opensource.org/license/mit"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">MIT</a
          >; catalog data under <a
            href="https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">CC0 1.0</a
          >.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Hand-built</strong> by <a
            href="https://github.com/Pratiyush"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">Pratiyush</a
          > — one maintainer, no team, no investors, no roadmap meetings.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="signoff">
        <span aria-hidden="true">— </span>End of Issue №{issueNumber}.
        <span class="signoff-mono">See you in 2027.</span>
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>

  <style>
    .annual-report {
      padding-top: var(--space-xl);
      padding-bottom: var(--space-2xl);
    }

    /* Masthead bar — lifted from index.astro hero. Mono uppercase, ink-faint, coral issue number. */
    .masthead {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 0.6em;
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: 0.72rem;
      letter-spacing: 0.16em;
      color: var(--color-ink-faint);
      margin: 0 0 var(--space-lg);
      padding-bottom: var(--space-sm);
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
      flex-wrap: wrap;
    }
    .masthead .issue {
      color: var(--color-coral);
      font-weight: 500;
    }
    .masthead .verified-line {
      color: var(--color-ink-soft);
      font-weight: 500;
    }
    .masthead .dot {
      color: var(--color-rule);
    }
    .masthead .rule {
      flex: 1;
      height: 1px;
      background: var(--color-rule);
      min-width: 1em;
    }
    .masthead .updated {
      color: var(--color-ink-faint);
    }

    /* Eyebrow — same pattern as legal.astro. */
    .rom-eyebrow {
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: 0.72rem;
      letter-spacing: 0.16em;
      color: var(--color-ink-faint);
      margin: 0 0 var(--space-sm);
      display: flex;
      align-items: baseline;
      gap: 0.5em;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
    }
    .rom {
      color: var(--color-coral);
      font-family: var(--font-display);
      font-style: italic;
      font-size: 1.1em;
    }
    .dot {
      color: var(--color-rule);
    }

    /* H1 — italic Fraunces, oversized. */
    .annual-report h1 {
      font-family: var(--font-display);
      font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 4.4vw, 3.2rem);
      font-weight: 400;
      font-style: italic;
      letter-spacing: -0.015em;
      margin: 0 0 var(--space-md);
      line-height: 1.05;
      max-width: 18ch;
    }

    /* Lede — larger Fraunces, ink color, capped at 58ch so the eye doesn't run. */
    .report-head .lede {
      font-family: var(--font-display);
      font-size: 1.18rem;
      font-weight: 350;
      color: var(--color-ink);
      line-height: 1.55;
      margin: 0 0 var(--space-2xl);
      max-width: 60ch;
    }
    .report-head .lede em {
      font-style: italic;
      color: var(--color-ink);
    }

    /* Section blocks — top rule, ~65ch readable column. */
    .block {
      padding: var(--space-lg) 0;
      border-top: var(--border-rule);
      max-width: 65ch;
    }
    .block h2 {
      font-family: var(--font-display);
      font-size: 1.45rem;
      font-weight: 500;
      margin: 0 0 var(--space-md);
      display: flex;
      align-items: baseline;
      gap: 0.5em;
      line-height: 1.2;
    }
    .block p {
      line-height: 1.65;
      margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
      color: var(--color-ink);
    }
    .block a {
      color: var(--color-ink);
      text-decoration: underline;
      text-decoration-color: var(--color-coral);
    }
    .block em {
      font-style: italic;
    }
    .section-blurb {
      color: var(--color-ink-soft);
      max-width: 60ch;
    }

    /* Entry — a single editorial blurb (h3 + paragraph), separated by a hairline rule. */
    .entry {
      margin-top: var(--space-md);
      padding-top: var(--space-md);
      border-top: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    }
    .entry:first-of-type {
      border-top: none;
      padding-top: 0;
    }
    .entry h3 {
      font-family: var(--font-display);
      font-size: 1.1rem;
      font-weight: 500;
      font-style: italic;
      margin: 0 0 0.3rem;
      color: var(--color-ink);
      letter-spacing: -0.005em;
    }
    .entry p {
      margin: 0;
      line-height: 1.65;
    }

    /* Big-number stat grid — Fraunces numerals, mono labels, same pattern as index hero-stats. */
    .stats-block {
      max-width: 65ch;
    }
    .report-stats {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
      gap: var(--space-lg) var(--space-lg);
      margin: var(--space-md) 0 0;
      padding: var(--space-md);
      background: var(--color-paper-warm);
      border: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
      border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    }
    @media (max-width: 640px) {
      .report-stats {
        grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      }
    }
    .report-stats .stat {
      min-width: 0;
    }
    .report-stats .stat-wide {
      grid-column: 1 / -1;
    }
    .report-stats .stat dt {
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: 0.68rem;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.1em;
      color: var(--color-ink-faint);
    }
    .report-stats .stat dd {
      margin: 0.2rem 0 0.3rem;
      font-family: var(--font-display);
      font-size: 2.4rem;
      font-weight: 400;
      color: var(--color-ink);
      line-height: 1;
      font-feature-settings: 'tnum';
      letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    }
    .report-stats .stat dd .unit {
      font-size: 0.55em;
      color: var(--color-ink-faint);
      margin-left: 0.18em;
      font-feature-settings: normal;
      letter-spacing: 0;
    }
    .report-stats .stat-note {
      margin: 0;
      font-size: 0.85rem;
      color: var(--color-ink-soft);
      line-height: 1.45;
      max-width: 36ch;
    }

    /* Colophon — definition-list shape, almanac sign-off. */
    .colophon-list {
      list-style: none;
      padding: 0;
      margin: 0 0 var(--space-lg);
    }
    .colophon-list li {
      padding-left: 1.4em;
      text-indent: -1.4em;
      line-height: 1.55;
      margin-bottom: 0.45rem;
      color: var(--color-ink);
    }
    .colophon-list li::before {
      content: '— ';
      color: var(--color-coral);
    }
    .colophon-list em {
      font-style: italic;
    }
    .signoff {
      font-family: var(--font-display);
      font-style: italic;
      font-size: 1.05rem;
      color: var(--color-ink-soft);
      margin: var(--space-md) 0 0;
      padding-top: var(--space-md);
      border-top: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    }
    .signoff-mono {
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-style: normal;
      font-size: 0.72rem;
      letter-spacing: 0.16em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--color-ink-faint);
      margin-left: 0.4em;
    }

    code {
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: 0.88em;
      padding: 0.1rem 0.3rem;
      background: var(--color-paper-warm);
      border-radius: 3px;
    }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
